.TH PRINTF 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
printf \- format and write data
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B printf
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I format
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B printf
writes
.I format
to standard output, with each conversion specification in it replaced by
the next
.I argument
converted as the specification says.
It reads no input, and it adds no newline of its own.
.PP
Ordinary characters in the format are written as they are.
A backslash starts an escape sequence, which is replaced by the octet it
names; see
.BR "Escape sequences" .
A percent sign starts a conversion specification; see
.BR Conversions .
.PP
When the format has been written and arguments remain, the format is
used again from its start with the remaining arguments, until none
remain.
A pass that uses no argument, such as the only pass of a format with no
conversion specification, ends this repetition, and arguments left over
are ignored.
When the arguments run out partway through a pass, the rest of that pass
still runs and each remaining conversion uses an empty argument; see
.BR Arguments .
With no arguments at all, the format is written once in the same way.
.PP
The kit's shell has
.B printf
as a builtin that runs this same code, with the differences listed under
.BR NOTES ;
see
.BR sh (1).
Outside the shell, the
.I printf.exe
launcher runs the
.B busybox
applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SS Escape sequences
A backslash in the format, or in an argument converted by
.BR %b ,
starts an escape sequence, which is replaced as follows.
.TP
.B \ea
Alert (BEL, 0x07).
.TP
.B \eb
Backspace (0x08).
.TP
.B \ec
Stop: write nothing more, not even the rest of the format.
Output from before it is kept, the remaining arguments are not used, and
the exit status is not affected.
.TP
.B \ee
Escape (0x1B).
.TP
.B \ef
Form feed (0x0C).
.TP
.B \en
Line feed (0x0A).
.TP
.B \er
Carriage return (0x0D).
.TP
.B \et
Tab (0x09).
.TP
.B \ev
Vertical tab (0x0B).
.TP
.B \e\e
Backslash.
.TP
.BI \e ooo
The octet whose octal value is
.IR ooo ,
one to three digits.
A value above 255 ends the sequence before the digit that would exceed
it, so
.B \e400
is a space (octal 40) followed by
.BR 0 .
.IP
In the format, a leading 0 counts as one of the three digits, so
.B \e0101
is a backspace (octal 10) followed by
.BR 1 .
In an argument converted by
.BR %b ,
a 0 right after the backslash is not counted when an octal digit
follows it, so
.B \e0101
is
.BR A .
A
.B \e0
with no octal digit after it is a NUL octet in both.
.TP
.BI \ex HH
The octet whose hexadecimal value is
.IR HH ,
one or two digits in either case.
A
.B \ex
not followed by a hexadecimal digit is written as it is.
.PP
A backslash followed by any other character, or ending the format or an
argument, is written as it is, and the character after it is then read
normally.
So
.B \e\(dq
and
.B \eu0041
are written unchanged.
.SS Conversions
A conversion specification is a percent sign followed by these parts, in
order, with only the conversion character required:
.PP
.RS 4
\fB%\fR[\fIflags\fR][\fIwidth\fR][\fB.\fIprecision\fR][\fIlength\fR]\fIconv\fR
.RE
.PP
The flags are any number of the following, in any order.
.TP
.B \-
Left-justify the result within the width.
.TP
.B +
Write a sign, either
.B +
or
.BR \- ,
with a signed number.
.TP
A space
Write a space before a non-negative signed number.
.TP
.B #
Use the alternate form: a leading
.B 0
for
.BR o ,
.B 0x
or
.B 0X
before a nonzero
.B x
or
.BR X ,
and a decimal point in floating-point output even when no digit follows
it.
.TP
.B 0
Pad with leading zeros instead of spaces.
This also applies to
.B c
and
.BR s .
.PP
The width is a minimum field width, as decimal digits or as
.BR * ,
which takes it from the next argument, a decimal integer.
A negative width from
.B *
is the
.B \-
flag with the width made positive.
.PP
The precision is a period followed by decimal digits or by
.BR * ,
or just a period, which means 0.
For integers it is the minimum number of digits, for
.BR e ,
.BR E ,
and
.B f
the digits after the decimal point, for
.B g
and
.B G
the significant digits, and for
.B s
the maximum number of bytes written.
The default for floating-point conversions is 6.
.PP
Any number of the length modifiers
.BR l ,
.BR L ,
.BR h ,
and
.B z
are accepted and ignored: integer conversions always use 64 bits.
.PP
The conversion character is one of the following.
.TP
.BR d ", " i
Write a signed decimal integer.
.TP
.BR o ", " u ", " x ", " X
Write an unsigned integer in octal, decimal, lowercase hexadecimal, or
uppercase hexadecimal.
.TP
.BR e ", " E
Write a floating-point number with an exponent, such as
.BR 1.234568e+004 ,
with
.B E
in place of
.B e
for
.BR E .
.TP
.B f
Write a floating-point number without an exponent, such as
.BR 1234.568000 .
.TP
.BR g ", " G
Write a floating-point number in the style of
.BR f ,
or in that of
.B e
or
.B E
when the exponent is below \-4 or not below the precision, with trailing
zeros removed unless the
.B #
flag is given.
.TP
.B c
Write the first byte of the argument.
An empty or missing argument writes a NUL octet.
A
.B *
width or precision does not work here: the byte's value becomes the width
or precision, and an unpredictable byte is written in its place.
.TP
.B s
Write the argument.
.TP
.B b
Write the argument with its escape sequences interpreted.
The percent sign must be followed by the
.B b
at once: flags, width, and precision are not accepted.
.TP
.B %
Write a percent sign.
As with
.BR b ,
nothing may come between the two percent signs.
.PP
Any other conversion character, such as
.BR a ,
.BR A ,
.BR F ,
.BR n ,
.BR p ,
or
.BR j ,
or a specification that ends before its conversion character, such as a
lone
.B %
at the end of the format, is an error: the message
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
printf: \fIspec\fR: invalid format
.EE
.RE
.PP
is written to standard error, where
.I spec
is the rest of the format from the percent sign with any length
modifiers removed, and
.B printf
stops with status 1.
Output from before the bad specification is kept.
Positional arguments such as
.B %1$s
and the
.B \(aq
flag are not supported.
.SS Arguments
Whatever the conversion, an argument that was not given is empty.
.PP
For
.BR d ,
.BR i ,
.BR o ,
.BR u ,
.BR x ,
and
.BR X ,
an argument is an integer in decimal, in hexadecimal with a
.B 0x
prefix, or in octal with a leading
.BR 0 ,
optionally signed and optionally preceded by white space.
Nothing may follow it.
For
.B d
and
.B i
the range is \-2^63 to 2^63\-1.
For the others it is 0 to 2^64\-1, and a negative number down to \-2^63
is accepted as its two's complement, so
.B %u
of
.B \-1
is
.BR 18446744073709551615 .
An empty or missing argument is an error here.
.PP
For
.BR e ,
.BR E ,
.BR f ,
.BR g ,
and
.BR G ,
an argument is a number in the form that the C library's
.B strtod
function reads: decimal with an optional exponent, hexadecimal such as
.BR 0x1p3 ,
.BR inf ,
or
.BR nan ,
optionally signed and optionally preceded by white space.
Nothing may follow it.
An empty or missing argument is 0, not an error.
.PP
For all of these, an argument may instead be a single or double quote
followed by a character, whose value is that of the first byte after the
quote, so
.B \(aqA
and
.B \(dqA
are both 65.
Anything after that byte is ignored, and a lone quote is 0.
A character above U+007F gives the first byte of its encoding, which is
UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and later; see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
An argument that is not a valid number, or that is out of range, is an
error: the message
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
printf: invalid number \(aq\fIargument\fR\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
is written to standard error, output continues, and the exit status will
be 1.
The value used is 0, not the number that began the argument, except that
an out-of-range floating-point argument keeps the value that
.B strtod
gave it.
.PP
An argument taken by
.B *
as a width or precision is a decimal integer from \-2^31 to 2^31\-1,
optionally preceded by a minus sign.
White space, a plus sign, a
.B 0x
prefix, or a quote makes it invalid.
An invalid one is reported the same way and is 0, but does not change the
exit status.
A missing one is 0 without a message.
A negative precision counts as none.
.SH OPTIONS
.B printf
has no options.
A first argument that starts with a dash is the format, so
.B "printf \-n"
writes
.BR \-n .
Two arguments are recognized.
.TP
.B \-\-
As the first argument, ends the options and is skipped, so that a format
that starts with a dash can follow it.
Only one is skipped: in
.BR "printf \-\- \-\- x" ,
the format is
.BR \-\- .
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument of the applet, writes a usage message on standard
output and exits with status 0, whatever else is given.
The shell's builtin has no
.BR \-\-help :
there it is a format, so
.B "printf \-\-help"
writes
.BR \-\-help .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
the applet expands wildcards in its arguments, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe ;
see
.BR NOTES .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
arguments as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ", " BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION
When standard output is a console, decide whether the console or busybox
interprets the escape sequences in the output, such as for color.
See
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B CONEMUPID
When it is set and neither of the above is, busybox uses terminal mode
0: it turns virtual terminal output off and emulates escape sequences
itself.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including output stopped by
.BR \ec .
.TP
.B 1
An argument was not a valid number, or a conversion specification was
not valid.
This is the status even if
.B \ec
stopped the output later.
It is also the status of a missing format in the applet.
.TP
.B 2
A missing format in the shell's builtin.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader closed it before all the output
was written.
.PP
A missing format in the applet writes the usage message to standard
error.
In the builtin the message is
.BR "usage: printf FORMAT [ARGUMENT...]" ,
preceded by the shell's name and a colon.
.SH EXAMPLES
Write each argument on a line of its own, using the format once for each:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq%s\en\(aq one two three
one
two
three
.EE
.RE
.PP
Align columns:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq%\-10s %5d\en\(aq apple 3 banana 12
apple          3
banana        12
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert a hexadecimal number to decimal, and a decimal number to
hexadecimal:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq%d %#x\en\(aq 0xff 255
255 0xff
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the code of a character:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq%d\en\(aq "\(aqA"
65
.EE
.RE
.PP
Take the field width from an argument:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq%*d\en\(aq 6 42
    42
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a number with two decimals and a percent sign:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq%.2f%%\en\(aq 12.345
12.35%
.EE
.RE
.PP
Interpret the escapes in one argument but not in another:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq%s|%b|\en\(aq \(aqa\ex41\(aq \(aqa\ex41\(aq
a\ex41|aA|
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a file with CR LF line endings:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqfirst\er\ensecond\er\en\(aq >dos.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a word in red on a console:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq\e033[31mred\e033[0m\en\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write text that
.BR echo (1)
would take as an option:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq%s\en\(aq "$text"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Pass file names, which may contain spaces, to a program that reads names
separated by NUL octets:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq%s\e0\(aq *.c | xargs \-0 wc \-c
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.B printf
and bash:
.IP \(bu 2
A missing argument for an integer conversion is an error, with exit
status 1, where they use 0.
.IP \(bu 2
A numeric argument with trailing characters is an error, and its value
is 0, instead of the number that began it.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \e\(dq
is a backslash and a double quote, and there are no
.B \eu
or
.B \eU
escapes.
.IP \(bu 2
There are no
.BR %a ,
.BR %A ,
or
.B %F
conversions, and none of bash's
.BR %q ,
.BR "%(...)T" ,
or
.B \-v
.IR var .
.PP
The builtin differs from the applet in the exit status of a missing
format, in having no
.BR \-\-help ,
and in the name that starts its error messages, which is the shell's,
such as
.BR sh ,
instead of
.BR printf .
In the shell the builtin runs in preference to the applet and to any
program named
.B printf
in
.BR PATH .
.PP
Numbers are written by the C runtime, which differs from glibc.
Exponents have three digits, as in
.BR 1.000000e+000 .
Infinity and not-a-number are written as forms such as
.B 1.#INF00
and
.BR 1.#QNAN0 .
An exact half rounds away from zero, so
.B %.0f
of 2.5 is 3.
The decimal point is always a period.
.PP
Standard output is binary, so
.B \en
is a bare LF.
Write
.B \er\en
for CR LF.
Output is written in blocks: one at the end of each pass through the
format, or sooner when a newline arrives with more than 40 bytes
pending.
A write error is not detected, except that a closed pipe ends
.B printf
with status 141.
Error messages are not buffered, so when standard output and standard
error go to the same place, a message can come before output that
preceded it.
.PP
In a batch file,
.B cmd.exe
treats the percent sign specially: write each
.B %
as
.BR %% .
Unless
.B BB_GLOBBING
is
.BR 0 ,
as it is under the kit's shell, the C runtime of the applet expands
wildcards in the format and the arguments, even quoted ones.
So when
.B printf.exe
is started from
.BR cmd.exe ,
a format or argument with
.B *
or
.B ?\&
that matches file names is replaced by them.
Set
.B BB_GLOBBING
to
.B 0
to prevent that.
.PP
Arguments may contain any Unicode character on Windows 10 version 1903
and later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
Widths and precisions count bytes, so a precision can cut a UTF-8
character in two.
When standard output is a console, the output is converted for display
from the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later.
Escape sequences, such as those for color, take effect, and busybox
emulates them on consoles without virtual terminal support.
A block of output that contains a NUL octet, from
.BR \e0 ,
.BR \ex00 ,
or
.BR %c ,
is written to a console unconverted and without escape sequence
emulation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR echo (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR xargs (1)
